{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And a priest's daughter, etc. — she, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they derive from the restriction of the word ״הִיא״ to her mother: that her mother eats from her [portion], even though she herself does not eat, and even if her mother was widowed." "And I have seen fit to awaken the hearts of the children of men with a great allusion that this pericah hinted at. Know that they of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 93a) that the nation of Israel — their level is above the level of the angels. And they further said (Zohar 2:155a) that HaShem created four worlds, one above the other, and they are inscribed in the verse (Isaiah 43:7): ״כֹּל הַנִּקְרָא בִשְׁמִי וְלִכְבוֹדִי בְּרָאתִיו יְצַרְתִּיו אַף עֲשִׂיתִיו״ — לִכְבוֹדִי, the supreme world called Atzilut; בְּרָאתִיו, that called the world of Beriah; יְצַרְתִּיו, Yetzirah; עֲשִׂיתִיו, Asiyah. Further, you must know that His glory, may He be blessed, fills every single world, and even beneath a world, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:9): ״וּמִתַּחַת זְרֹעֹת עוֹלָם״, and His light, may He be blessed, illuminates in the four worlds — this is in the sod of the four letters of the name HaVaYaH, may He be blessed: yod in the world of Atzilut, the first heh in the world of Beriah, and so on. And they of blessed memory said (Zohar 3:120b) that the vitality that moves in the children of El Hai is from His light, may He be blessed, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:27): ״כִּי חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״. HaShem also called them His sons." "<b>Further,</b> they of blessed memory said (Zohar 1:30b, 64a) that the aspects of the vitality in man are assigned four appellations: one is called nefesh, and one is called ruach, and one is called neshamah, and one is called chayah, in the sod of ״כִּי רוּחַ הַחַיָּה בָּאוֹפַנִּים״, and it is called neshamah le-neshamah. And the men of truth said that the aspect that comes from the light of the world of Asiyah — since it is slight and contains only a minimal portion of the moving vitality — is that which perceive  in the perceive  in the impressions of this world; and HaShem made it so for the sake of the world, because of its being coarse, and this is called nefesh. And that which comes from the light of the world of Yetzirah — since it is higher than the world of Asiyah, its illumination is great and it is called ruach. And that which comes from the light of the world of Beriah is called neshamah according to its level, and that which comes from the light of the world of Atzilut is called neshamah le-neshamah." "<b>And behold,</b> the Master, HaShem of Hosts, made a binding connection of all the worlds, spiritual and physical, in the upright man — He bound all corporeality and all spirituality together. The corporeal He built from four elements, and there He established for Himself the law of spirituality from all the worlds. And you must know that corporeality opposes the spiritual union more than the opposition of fire and water; therefore HaShem, may He be blessed, devised wisely and created in man the first station, which is the nefesh, which contains only a very slight portion of spirituality, and He lodged within it a higher aspect than it, which is the aspect of ruach. Thus the nefesh stands as intermediary between the body and the ruach, for the ruach does not abide in corporeality without the intermediary of the nefesh. And likewise the ruach mediates between the nefesh and the neshamah, for the nefesh relative to the neshamah is like the body relative to the ruach. And likewise neshamah mediates between the ruach and neshamah le-neshamah. You thus find that the nefesh can stand alone, and the ruach with two, and the neshamah with three, and neshamah le-neshamah with four." "<b>Further,</b> those who know sacred knowledge said (Zohar 2:94a) that the intensification of the perfected ones through the mediation of ״לֶקַח טוֹב״ — the good ״taking״ — that the Creator gave us is to ascend from the lower level to the level above it. On this path nefesh ascends to the level of ruach, and ruach to the level of neshamah, and neshamah to the level of neshamah le-neshamah. And this is the ultimate purpose of the cause of man's creation. Give to a wise man and he will grow wiser." "<b>Further,</b> they said, of blessed memory, that the apprehension of this spiritual ascent revolves around the idea that a person may send forth his hand and benefit as a man who acts with his own. And it is the sod of (Psalms 128:2) ״יְגִיעַ כַּפֶּיךָ כִּי תֹאכֵל אַשְׁרֶיךָ וְטוֹב לָךְ״ — meaning your joy and your good. And it is the statement of our Sages, of blessed memory, that the soul, before its entry into this world, eats as the daughter of a bogeret, and upon its return, as the daughter of a married woman." "Further, you should know that everything that YHWH formed and created and made — all stands for sustenance and nourishment from the supernal world, which is called the world of Atzilut, and it is the light of the yud in His name, blessed be He. And that sustenance is called kodesh (holy), and it is the source of vitality and joy and good. And although vitality descends to all created beings — for without vitality there is no created thing, and it is the sod of the verse (Nehemiah 9:6) ״וְאַתָּה מְחַיֶּה אֶת כֻּלָּם״ — with all this, the flow changes as it grows distant, just as that which grows from the earth changes when it is distanced from the source of nourishment. And YHWH did thus to give each one according to its level and according to what it attains through its purity; also to the physical earth, YHWH gives it water — and this is its flow and its vitality and its bringing forth and its growth. And in this manner likewise is the flow of the vital force of the nefashot and the ruchot and the neshamot and neshamot le-neshamot according to their level, for not all nefashot are at one level, and not all etc. are equal etc., and there is no created being — whether among the physical or the spiritual, whether among the supernal angels or among nefashot, ruchot (forces), neshamot, neshamot le-neshamot — that does not need the holy flow; rather, each one according to its holy root." "<b>Further,</b> you should know that since there is a person who merits all the holy levels, and there is one who merits three, and one who merits two, and one who merits one — and someone who merited only the nefesh in this world but not the ruach, his deeds are of no avail at all, for he has no power in his hand to return it on high, since he has no ability to direct it except through the mediation of the ruach. If he merits the ruach through the mediation of his good deeds, he elevates the nefesh to the level of the ruach. And know that when he elevates it to the level of the ruach, behold he has acquired it through his deeds and is called its master (ba'al), and it is the sod of the verse (Proverbs 23:2) ״אִם בַּעַל נֶפֶשׁ אָתָּה״ — meaning, you have acquired it and it is yours through the mediation of exertion, and that which is apprehended through exertion makes the ruach become ba'al of the nefesh, for it is an olah with him and they are equal in level — and the place of his stationing is the place of her stationing: מִפִּתּוֹ תֹּאכַל וּמִכּוֹסוֹ תִּשְׁתֶּה וּבְחֵיקוֹ תִּשְׁכָּב. And likewise the ruach in relation to the neshamah, and the neshamah in relation to its neshamah. And the acquisition of the level of holy aspects comes about through one of two causes: either from the side of the progenitor, for the father activates a supernal force in the son, as I explained in parashat Vayehi (Genesis 49:3) in the verse ״רְאוּבֵן בְּכֹרִי״; or through intensification by means of engagement in Torah, as is written in the holy Zohar (Zohar III, 91a) in the parashah of ״שׁוֹר אוֹ כֶשֶׂב אוֹ עֵז כִּי יִוָּלֵד״ — that a person can attain according to the measure of his intensification in Torah etc., even if he be at a level than which there is none lower, if he exerts himself he will attain supernal levels according to the measure of exertion; refer there to his words." "<b>Further,</b> you should know that the harm caused by a person's sin likewise changes according to the person's level, for the blemish that afflicts the nefesh is not like the blemish of the ruach and the other levels — for a blemish of the nefesh brings about its destruction, in the sod of (Ezekiel 18:4) ״הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַחוֹטֵאת הִיא תָּמוּת״, whereas a blemish of the ruach, even if it causes no more than discolorations and afflictions, is considered a great blemish. And therefore YHWH commands concerning it: ״וְהָרוּחַ תָּשׁוּב אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר נְתָנָהּ״, and our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Shabbat 152b): give it back as it was given to you — for otherwise the impurities of tumah cling to it and drag it to the regiments of the Other Side and prevent it from returning to its original place. And a blemish of the neshamah — although it does not reach the measure mentioned, but only diminishes its light from what it was — covers him with shame, for everything depends on the one who causes shame and the one who feels shame. And a blemish of neshamah le-neshamah — although no blemish reaches it and it does not diminish its light, but rather it causes it to depart from him against its will, as they said in the Tikkunim (Tikkun 70) in the sod of the verse ״לֹא יְאֻנֶּה לַצַּדִּיק כָּל אָוֶן״ — that Scripture speaks of one who possesses a neshamah le-neshamah, and when a person comes to sin, the holy neshamah departs so that no sin should befall it, and this departure aggrieves it according to the measure of its greatness. To what may the matter be likened? To a king's intimate companion in his house who then changes his mind and banishes him." "<b>And after</b> YHWH has informed us of all that has been said, we can say that these are indeed the matters spoken of in the verses. And He began to say <b>וְכָל זָר לֹא יֹאכַל קֹדֶשׁ</b> — speaking of two matters of kodesh: of the kodesh of the offerings, and of the hidden kodesh, which is the spiritual flow, the sustenance of the children of Elohim." "And the verse says <b>a priest's resident and a hired laborer</b> to allude to the spirit (ruach) and to the soul (nefesh), for the spirit is a priest's resident — meaning, for in it is the dwelling of the soul (neshamah), which is called a priest, for it is the supernal rung; and a hired laborer is likewise the soul (nefesh), for it performs all the deeds of man. These two levels Scripture informs are not fit to eat of the sustenance called <i>kodesh</i>, on account of its being a great rung. Go forth and learn from what the Rashbi wrote (Zohar II, 121a) concerning the distinction between <i>kodesh</i> and <i>kodashim</i>." "And the verse says <b>and a priest, when he acquires a soul</b>, etc. — meaning: the supernal soul is called a priest. And it mentions it in the masculine to say that once it has reached this condition it is called by the rung of <i>zakhar</i> (male). ״When he acquires a soul״ — for through the medium of the merit of its deeds it also acquires the soul (nefesh) and elevates it to its rung, and it is called a master of soul (בעל נפשׁ) (Proverbs 23:2)." "<b>He may eat of it.</b> Meaning: that he merits to let the soul (nefesh) too benefit from <i>kodesh</i>, which was not so before its ascent. And it says the soul (nefesh), and all the more so the spirit (ruach), which is higher than the soul." "And the verse's saying <b>and one born in his house</b> alludes to souls of holiness that fell from primordial time, and they are refined through the medium of the merit of the righteous' deeds — for the good deed is like a drawing stone, to extract the good spark — and they become attributed under the name of those born of the soul (neshamah) that refines them. And Scripture says that they too ascend with it to benefit from <i>kodesh</i>." "And the verse's saying <b>and a priest's daughter</b> — Scripture speaks of the soul (neshamah) that became blemished and defiled through its deeds, such that it forfeits the benefit of <i>kodesh</i>. And this is the verse's saying <b>and a priest's daughter</b> — that is, the soul; <b>when she becomes belonging to a stranger</b> — that is Samael, whose enticements she lusted after and went astray after him — ״of the terumah of the holy things she may not eat.״ It was precise in saying ״of the terumah of,״ to say that even terumah of the holy things she may not eat — two in a hundred, and all the more so <i>kodesh</i> itself. And it was precise in saying ״she״ to exclude another case that follows from its ruling adjacent thereto, as it is written ״and a daughter״ (״ובת״). And this is the affliction of the soul when its sustenance is withheld: it is lowered and the light of its radiance darkens, and its splendor descends — may the Merciful One save us."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {5 "The rabbinic idiom 'כבת בוגרת / כבת נשואה' refers to the permission for a girl past puberty (bogeret) or a married woman (nesu'ah) to eat terumah; the verse from Psalms is understood as saying the soul partakes of its own portion.", 6 "The text contains abbreviated phrases 'ולא כל וכו׳ שווֹת וכו׳' ('and not all etc. are equal etc.'), apparently truncating a fuller statement about the unequal ranks of spiritual beings; rendered literally as they stand."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:18.925963046Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}